#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "89a4773c-f6bb-577d-8743-5d0a46c1da6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5573" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=1[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4985be13-9b44-5ec5-b342-00354339b902" :question/math-academy-id "e-5574" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-2)}^{2}+{(2-2)}^{2}+{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "82a0e170-d916-5ae7-bd72-9b47c7287e0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7173" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by   \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{13\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{13\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7014f0ec-9ce3-598a-8ddb-b71a000d383f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7309" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{10})}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\,\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10\\,\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fabdb13-e63a-5fac-8f0e-2adcc3b7c99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309354} {:answer/id #uuid "1882b753-ce87-5e1f-8c49-7f1045517fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309355} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7904f4-5641-5eb7-b15f-974967f3dae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309356} {:answer/id #uuid "2d736ac1-be96-5ff9-bd70-b9b2ec0445a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1882b753-ce87-5e1f-8c49-7f1045517fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309355} :answer-field/id #uuid "3111bcd7-e673-5843-b524-72001f890ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d784c99-ccfa-5cf1-aee8-af8e0a39bd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-140120" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{7\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{7\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2}{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3fcad1f-bae1-565b-a80f-2ebc8f80b415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41.39}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309360} {:answer/id #uuid "23a797f4-caec-5782-962a-29d0b880207f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309361} {:answer/id #uuid "951c7659-80e6-5d3a-8a35-d3a66f6a03e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309362} {:answer/id #uuid "a172fce5-781c-52a7-b0a0-1ec3570fa3bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.23}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309363} {:answer/id #uuid "d941431a-d217-59e5-b268-e77b23af5124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44.92}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951c7659-80e6-5d3a-8a35-d3a66f6a03e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f3fbaa-468c-5d47-b722-06597f0b0c58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "180e6dff-0b43-5239-b883-c2a6466ae49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140451" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{\\sqrt{53}\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{29}{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\frac{29}{53}})≈{42.29}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f1b3fc-6be1-512b-b0f0-ee87644a1650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309367} {:answer/id #uuid "d16d7909-b67c-5eb3-954f-655074d227cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309368} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6464a7-a537-5b6e-b252-b6091b1cf4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309369} {:answer/id #uuid "6733aca9-fc57-56c0-b3f3-b090ac5fe2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309370} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1dd863-5490-53e4-b835-2695e4378ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6733aca9-fc57-56c0-b3f3-b090ac5fe2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309370} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf703a9-7b21-5692-bcf1-5dd4d1095a89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fa47428-4db5-5247-a146-29b4cebc836f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39992" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set \n${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075be246-5690-5600-83f3-b96410a0318b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309374} {:answer/id #uuid "43b8d445-4681-56ba-84a7-849ee344c769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309375} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eee616-86f4-5dd8-b88f-7e0f6ce3035b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309376} {:answer/id #uuid "7af44923-7c70-53fd-b44e-cff335edfcab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309377} {:answer/id #uuid "15ba719e-fad7-53c1-8697-1e50e2d39c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b8d445-4681-56ba-84a7-849ee344c769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309375} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eaa3455-cc89-537e-846b-9cdaca615870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8a18aba-5bf5-5598-9a5a-e92c825d1e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-40192" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29ebffe-7b4e-5d9f-bdba-5c1ecb74de9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131565} {:answer/id #uuid "18db8704-c3ae-55d9-aeec-2dc393a42bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131566} {:answer/id #uuid "305f7a56-e420-5e0a-aaa1-375007e53a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131567} {:answer/id #uuid "437131d3-209d-5440-8146-a7acea973eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131568} {:answer/id #uuid "6225371f-d2a8-508d-b6e3-8d38ebe91884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18db8704-c3ae-55d9-aeec-2dc393a42bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131566} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09a3b5d-acdb-524b-ab6f-c052153e160e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27fc7f62-58d5-5ef7-b202-bc3a925043e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40194" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b44947-9539-5b1f-acca-34c71abdca6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.98^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131631} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2d3eae-bddf-5eff-8893-81f28ac8d3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.98^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131632} {:answer/id #uuid "d88a7ed1-0d96-521f-add7-86dc8b7770a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.29^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131633} {:answer/id #uuid "dac94d56-de29-594a-ab9a-ee4f0b9e63b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.29^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131634} {:answer/id #uuid "4185edc9-b740-5617-b2c9-553d2d4b215b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.38^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b2d3eae-bddf-5eff-8893-81f28ac8d3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.98^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131632} :answer-field/id #uuid "307fd06c-e4cf-5e21-8b47-682acf46ca81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "961a93f3-4659-59c1-955c-0c479788bf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-40223" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,9\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 11+(-1)\\cdot (-7)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{11}^{2}+(-7)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{171}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9})≈{32.98}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba09dd52-71f4-5c94-8095-ce6179bcac47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309381} {:answer/id #uuid "112969a0-33d8-58a1-9802-8f2b6471ac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309382} {:answer/id #uuid "41ed7f1c-9bfd-58cd-9dcc-d1e5f2a4a9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} {:answer/id #uuid "a80b0efd-b15a-52dc-93bb-c977e521ec06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309384} {:answer/id #uuid "85ca9509-047a-504b-9974-fed193ebd32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112969a0-33d8-58a1-9802-8f2b6471ac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309382} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce69b82-5134-572b-bb60-370523024825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b083e078-533c-51be-b620-d91910a453ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-40605" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(0-0)^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1f3d28b-0f1e-58e6-82c1-ae4d7045308b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309388} {:answer/id #uuid "981c972c-defc-548f-b995-d1b9aea35858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309389} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf22801-d54d-5e74-98de-e82e9e5ce127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309390} {:answer/id #uuid "27f6935f-508c-57aa-b056-934f150ceb45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309391} {:answer/id #uuid "43736521-2c65-5c31-8170-376728e41bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27f6935f-508c-57aa-b056-934f150ceb45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309391} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbf7121-a3bf-5540-8737-ec00639e00dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6f714f8-ee02-5c1a-90d6-e67b1ec76974" :question/math-academy-id "q-40610" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-\\frac{2}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ coincides with the projection, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is zero $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a90e3e1f-e050-52bd-990f-e3f26a1b1809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309395} {:answer/id #uuid "f75421ab-4941-5c0f-a101-8291a450cd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309396} {:answer/id #uuid "032a1e0b-f5a5-5232-9b50-912d7e3593f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309397} {:answer/id #uuid "6807ceac-729e-5bb7-8fe7-460e6a4b1089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309398} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4a357b-20b4-54bd-9acb-8502b534faae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90e3e1f-e050-52bd-990f-e3f26a1b1809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309395} :answer-field/id #uuid "199b6905-551a-5894-85d9-e2418be61ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3affa7c6-8db6-53c2-af21-9110c8fb933c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40615" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfb16e53-12f3-57be-8f05-0eb43be26d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309402} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee825fb-2262-52ee-a613-729ebef83676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309403} {:answer/id #uuid "1d89d14d-ba40-5f49-a20e-8a35bc5ad9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309404} {:answer/id #uuid "7672db66-6f55-5ab7-8cc3-c49cfdcfe04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309405} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd36e6d-6eb5-5d7e-8b06-181a5364f123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ee825fb-2262-52ee-a613-729ebef83676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309403} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc19ae9-e7f1-5aa1-bbcc-d9d381f71221" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afc5be3a-a134-55b4-b40c-0093014944dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40616" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{20\\cdot 4+(-20)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 0}{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{20\\cdot 5+(-20)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 0}{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{41}(-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fb0a5a2-2274-5cfb-8f2d-602cbbd79f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131823} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0a7f55-45e5-5f46-8b53-b50859450b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131824} {:answer/id #uuid "af8a0ed9-a5be-54da-9f9b-3bb41e112687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131825} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1bd418-3d75-5da2-a3c6-3b22a49d60a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131826} {:answer/id #uuid "18696492-ac15-5f85-90c0-0751255362cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1bd418-3d75-5da2-a3c6-3b22a49d60a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131826} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe92c549-b608-5faa-95c5-35694d31916c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d36e6e8-ef03-50a4-b033-e23e74e3f9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-40666" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84232768-408e-5e6c-8f79-61ccceb73140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309409} {:answer/id #uuid "8516c9e7-5f5e-547f-9678-281e8ff0dc70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309410} {:answer/id #uuid "ac92573d-f63f-5f41-9a03-f223047bd9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309411} {:answer/id #uuid "93f9c0f3-a652-5148-9ef1-32b5a0730a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309412} {:answer/id #uuid "99b8e110-d675-5a88-9e2e-dc5d325e01d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac92573d-f63f-5f41-9a03-f223047bd9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309411} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfcc758-2483-5389-8a77-c18a00e95769" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5dbbef3-e100-51de-9e04-d411f861eff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81472" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-12)\\cdot (-2)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-12)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-12)\\cdot (-2)}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58726918-637a-5bd6-a352-b101207d8960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309416} {:answer/id #uuid "769f6b06-b69b-5d58-b2be-03e8456e931b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309417} {:answer/id #uuid "fb278d51-2145-5623-aeda-71f3a8cc2127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309418} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1f0fbf-4f9b-5ab4-a3b5-983524ba85b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309419} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9f9ed7-5769-5250-8104-975d6bc3177a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1f0fbf-4f9b-5ab4-a3b5-983524ba85b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309419} :answer-field/id #uuid "548b53e5-748f-53fe-892f-f85925e4347e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f29dd616-20ae-5ac6-8d0f-2176557e28bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81585" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c903674e-4c5e-5eee-8c3a-985872b670f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309423} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c2a2b3-b19b-50cc-a272-87100636bc44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309424} {:answer/id #uuid "7d56cdac-99b0-57ba-b8bc-32cfcd3f97d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309425} {:answer/id #uuid "6d26669b-e75c-506f-af97-69586c350a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309426} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3e4556-8a8b-5d84-af81-06bfa3d41891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc3e4556-8a8b-5d84-af81-06bfa3d41891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309427} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44d0dd1-cce6-50e2-8924-dd49a012d838" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f8ae67f-2056-5306-a388-43fea1c795b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-81591" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=-1[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a5c59a-7294-5e50-a808-8270f997a1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309430} {:answer/id #uuid "75c6f8ae-0cc3-5e3b-94f7-e26b3a503329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309431} {:answer/id #uuid "32d0f948-46b4-5cc2-aec4-c62b11ab3bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309432} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5f1bca-6083-5b56-b7c2-c4fec7155deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309433} {:answer/id #uuid "d06f8674-1ce2-58c8-bec2-396eb596c055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75c6f8ae-0cc3-5e3b-94f7-e26b3a503329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309431} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f35744c-3349-5a16-94c5-d76c4aa38d80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c09fb10f-9709-56fe-a154-f4a9983b07c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81593" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 31\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-4)+31\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+31\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6183140a-d0a3-5b16-8b62-1a715d7809d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309437} {:answer/id #uuid "b84767b2-7d1c-5acc-93ff-e375f0d23667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309438} {:answer/id #uuid "d46e783b-a683-56c1-9d65-90f734b8ff25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309439} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9cb25b-354c-5882-8cd0-e89b0639dcf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309440} {:answer/id #uuid "cebb530e-4393-5181-a745-65b4bff1d4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6183140a-d0a3-5b16-8b62-1a715d7809d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309437} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ecc4bc-3bae-5db7-844a-545d6b3b3505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d063e51-f567-59de-8718-4d9cf3ca1740" :question/math-academy-id "q-82914" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2-\\frac{9}{4})}^{2}+{(1-(-\\frac{3}{4}))}^{2}+{(-3-(-\\frac{7}{4}))}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{49}{16}+\\frac{25}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{76}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4186be12-2e1c-5b44-8691-8b57f89175bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309444} {:answer/id #uuid "f420911b-16d3-5270-94ba-f8f7fbdd508a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309445} {:answer/id #uuid "36245817-c0b9-529e-a7f2-fb154a74b2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309446} {:answer/id #uuid "7275dc04-817d-58cf-9ea2-db78f97b35d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309447} {:answer/id #uuid "06a21957-878b-5e5f-b0cd-588057cab18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a21957-878b-5e5f-b0cd-588057cab18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309448} :answer-field/id #uuid "54da01ee-92bb-504f-a5c1-55e6114c3b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "852e8788-cba4-5ef6-96d9-b4cd2873381e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82915" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ coincides with the projection, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is zero $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f792ae-b8e3-5c21-b8bc-334dafbcd4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{12.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309451} {:answer/id #uuid "3b89cb54-e767-5c5d-a3b1-305b7020c9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309452} {:answer/id #uuid "0cdd4fb5-01e2-5e6b-ba98-a4c8714c3db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309453} {:answer/id #uuid "d668d40c-9a28-5184-8d6e-aaabf53684c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{11.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309454} {:answer/id #uuid "98d71906-8c05-5a15-9ec9-e20a94433d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b89cb54-e767-5c5d-a3b1-305b7020c9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309452} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8bd3fd2-e289-5311-9140-b6f71db70b14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4417e099-8677-544d-a57a-1a6fa347aa4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82948" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{44}\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2\\sqrt{11}\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11})≈{25.24}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f808610-b7e8-56f4-af93-cc794fb8f85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309458} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7673a1-7656-52e5-b18e-02ccad4619f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309459} {:answer/id #uuid "940ef9bc-57fc-5858-a0d8-37f54a7c839c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309460} {:answer/id #uuid "afcf965c-e3e6-56a6-a1a5-556324362cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309461} {:answer/id #uuid "13ea00ec-9fae-5abe-a8a0-d727df109e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ea00ec-9fae-5abe-a8a0-d727df109e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309462} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6bea67-d5f4-5664-859b-bd9e4a8a8d97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d727048-e0d7-5a77-995e-bf03ee0a89fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-82950" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{5\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84231ab7-3deb-56ca-9b02-7524cf5f2438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309465} {:answer/id #uuid "a671535e-89de-550b-9fc8-7c0daf746afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48.59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309466} {:answer/id #uuid "dedd7b54-2e2b-57f4-abb5-e501bfdd0fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309467} {:answer/id #uuid "5de29845-916b-50af-82aa-d5deed981db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29.91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309468} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ec700-ce94-540c-9c34-3f1a5de9a83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7.18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84231ab7-3deb-56ca-9b02-7524cf5f2438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309465} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b989f56-c98e-5541-8090-aafb254c5786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a040e083-d81c-5acb-84bf-c293088be346" :question/math-academy-id "q-82978" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}]}
